{"files": {"SKILL.md": "# Working In A Terminal Sandbox\n\nFiles on disk are the deliverable. Narration does not score.\n\n## Write the deliverable on your second action\n\nWrite a first version to the exact named path early \u2014 before exploring alternatives. A partial file at the correct path scores; no file scores zero. Scratch and note directories are not delivery locations \u2014 write only to the path the task names.\n\n## Read the request \u2014 and the check it points at\n\nRead the instruction and any named test file in parallel. Extract: exact output path, format constraints, what the assertions check. Target the assertions, not your inference about what \"done\" means.\n\n## Each command is a question; its output is the answer\n\nIssue one batch, read the result in full, then decide. Tool stalls \u2192 write code to a temp file, run via terminal instead.\n\n## Ground claims in observable output\n\nDon't write values you haven't observed. Guessed or pattern-extrapolated values fail specific-value checks just as a missing file does. If data cannot be recovered, output only what you verified \u2014 partial truth outscores confident fiction.\n\n## Verify by running, not by reading\n\n`def test_*` files need `pytest` run from the correct directory, not `python`. Silent exit 0 from a runner that printed nothing means it ran nothing. If the tests directory contains a non-pytest driver script with CLI flags, run it directly with those flags \u2014 that is what the verifier will run.\n\nNever declare a test passed without running it.\n\nFor compiled programs: compile immediately after writing. After running, read back the output file and confirm the first and last characters match the expected format \u2014 an off-by-one byte fails exact-equality checks.\n\nTwo same-approach failures \u2192 change mechanism structurally on the third attempt.\n\n## The verifier may be stricter than anything you can see\n\nThe local check is a hint, not the contract. Prefer solutions robust across implementations \u2014 avoid relying on the specific weakness in the local copy.\n\n## Match what was named\n\nExact path, exact field names, exact format. Copy quoted signatures character-for-character including `async`, `def`, `static`. Numbers, size limits, and exact literals in the instruction are requirements, not illustrations.\n\n## Build tasks versus discovery tasks\n\n**Reconstruction**: write a minimal stub to the output path first (locks in exists/compiles/runs credit before any analysis). Do not embed data arrays \u2014 violates size and no-external-dependency constraints. Then iterate generate \u2192 compare \u2192 adjust.\n\n**Recovery**: copy source files BEFORE any recovery attempt \u2014 database engines, journaled stores, and archive utilities may consume or truncate the source on open (even read-only operations can trigger this). After copying, inspect raw bytes. Try format-specific recovery utilities first. If encoded, derive the transform from known plaintext at a fixed offset. Partial recovery scores higher than no file.\n\n**Binary analysis**: run static and dynamic analysis tools in parallel. Pipe the result directly to the output path \u2014 do not transcribe through prose.\n\n**Filter or sanitizer bypass**: the filter pattern-matches text; the consumer parses structure. The bypass lives where the two disagree. **In modern browsers, `javascript:` URLs in `<a>`, `<form action>`, `<button formaction>`, `<base href>`, `<meta refresh>`, and CSS `url(...)` either require user interaction or are blocked outright \u2014 these are dead ends.** Auto-trigger paths are elements/attributes that execute on parse alone.\n\nThe strongest filter-parser disagreements live at **delimited-region boundaries** \u2014 regions the filter treats as opaque data while the parser tokenizes structure inside them. A regex-based filter scans the document linearly as text and respects bracketed data regions and comment markers as literal data; it skips inside them rather than stripping. The HTML parser, however, follows different tokenization rules in different contexts: foreign content (inside `<svg>` and `<math>`) switches to XML-style parsing, where bracketed data regions, comments, and tag boundaries are interpreted differently than in the outer HTML body. That asymmetry means a region the filter reads as a single data blob can re-tokenize on the consumer's side into separate structural elements \u2014 including a fresh blocked tag that the filter never saw as such, because the filter's regex passed over it.\n\nProbe in this order: a foreign-content wrapper element containing a bracketed data region whose contents are **a closing tag matching that same wrapper element followed by an opening tag of a blocked element**. The data region is opaque to the filter (it skips inside) but re-tokenized by the parser, where the matching closing tag actually ends the wrapper's scope \u2014 freeing the blocked tag to live in the outer context. Then continue with character-entity-encoded delimiters inside SVG attributes, case-folded variants of blocked tags, and quoted-string boundaries the filter respects but the parser doesn't. **The matching of the closing tag to the wrapper element is the key \u2014 closing a different element inside the data region does not end the wrapper's scope, so the trapped blocked tag stays trapped.** **Try several structurally different shapes back-to-back. `javascript:`-URL approaches are exhausted; pivot to delimited-region experiments quickly.**\n\n**Async cleanup**: configure the concurrency runner so it awaits every task's cleanup (including awaits inside finally blocks) before propagating the cancellation signal.\n\n## When the deliverable runs elsewhere\n\nScripts run in a fresh container \u2014 install dependencies, write configs, start services inside the script. No init system: start daemons with their own binary. Verify the service is reachable before the script exits.\n\nRewrite configuration files rather than patching. Never use `sed` to insert into `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`: a `}` pattern matches the `events { }` closing brace first. To write the full nginx.conf from a shell script without shell-expanding `$nginx_variables`, use a single-quoted heredoc delimiter: `cat > /etc/nginx/nginx.conf << 'NGINX_EOF'`. Inside a single-quoted heredoc, `$` is passed literally \u2014 not expanded by bash. Place `log_format` and `limit_req_zone` inside the `http { }` block; server blocks go in `conf.d/`.\n\nContainer image assets are available in the verifier container too.\n\n## Install what the work needs\n\nUse the file tool for content with `$`, backticks, or quotes \u2014 shell heredocs and echoes silently corrupt them.\n\n## What \"done\" means\n\nPath exists; non-empty; structurally valid; test runner confirms it. If the instruction specifies a count, check your output count against it before stopping. If it specifies a numeric range, scan for out-of-range values \u2014 an invalid value crashes the parser before any logic is tested.\n\n## What to refuse\n\nDon't invent values not observed in the workspace. Don't declare tests passed without running them. Don't modify checks to force a pass.\n\nA partial answer at the named path scores above an unwritten one.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}